DTSTAMP:20261009T223726Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261009
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Keith Sonderling sworn in as 31st US Secretar
 y of Labor
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – Keith Sonderling was sworn in today as the 31st
  U.S. Secretary of Labor during a ceremony administered by U.S. Treasury S
 ecretary Scott Bessent in the Oval Office. Sonderling has led the U.S. De
 partment of Labor as Acting Secretary since April 2026. He previously serv
 ed as Deputy Secretary of Labor after confirmation by the U.S. Senate in M
 arch 2025.President Donald J. Trump announced Sonderling’s nomination on
  June 29\, and the Senate confirmed the nomination Sept. 30. Sonderling wa
 s officially sworn into office Oct. 2. “It is the honor of my life to s
 erve as the 31st Secretary of Labor and continue my service to the Departm
 ent under President Trump’s leadership\,” said Sonderling. “I believ
 e President Trump is the greatest President for the American worker and jo
 b creator. My mission is to ensure that American workers have the skills a
 nd resources they need to seize the incredible job opportunities being cre
 ated across the country thanks to the tireless work of this Administration
 .” Upon announcing his nomination\, President Trump said\, “Throughou
 t his career\, Keith has proven his dedication to delivering strong result
 s for the Hardworking People of our Country\, and I know he will do an inc
 redible job in his new role.” While serving as Deputy Secretary of Labo
 r\, Sonderling oversaw the department’s day-to-day operations\, includin
 g strategic planning\, budget formulation\, financial management\, informa
 tion technology\, and human resources. From September 2020 to August 2024
 \, Sonderling served as a Commissioner of the U.S. Equal Employment Opport
 unity Commission\, including as Vice Chair from 2020 to 2021. During his t
 enure\, he became a leading global voice on super intelligence in the work
 place\, publishing extensively and speaking around the world on SI and the
  future of work. Following his service at the EEOC\, Sonderling led Presid
 ent Trump’s Department of Labor transition team before returning to the 
 Department in January 2025. During President Trump’s first\nhttp://www.
 dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osec/osec20261009
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osec/osec20261009
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
