UID:7cdf5e7a01edcc6b@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261007T230622Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261007
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor cites Pennsylvania exc
 avation employer for failing to provide trench protection following fatal 
 trench cave-in
DESCRIPTION:CHAMBERSBURG\, PA – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited
  an excavation contractor for willful and serious safety violations after 
 a young worker was fatally injured in a trench collapse. The department
 ’s Occupational Safety and Health Administration opened an investigation
  into Jonas David Wagoner – doing business as J.D. Wagoner Excavating 
 – on April 2\, 2026\, and found workers were installing a storm sewer sy
 stem 12 feet below ground level in a trench that was not adequately protec
 ted from soil collapse. When a portion of the trench's wall failed\, the 
 unrestrained soil engulfed one worker. The agency issued a willful violati
 on for failure to protect workers from cave-ins and two repeat violations 
 for failing to assign a competent person to conduct daily trench inspectio
 ns and removing employees from hazardous conditions.In addition\, the empl
 oyer received three serious violations for failure to provide construction
  hazard prevention training to employees\, establish a safe means for work
 ers to exit the excavation site\, keep materials at least two feet from th
 e edge of an excavation site\, and restrain side materials from becoming r
 oll-in hazards.   OSHA proposed $254\,884 in penalties. The employer ha
 s 15 business days from receiving receipt of its citations and penalties t
 o request an informal conference with OSHA’s area director or contest th
 e findings before the independent Occupational Safety and Health Review Co
 mmission. Please check the OSHA establishment search page periodically for
  any changes in the inspection or penalty status.Visit OSHA’s website to
  learn about keeping workers safe around trenching and excavations. Employ
 ers can also contact the agency for information about OSHA’s compliance
  assistance resources and for free help on complying with OSHA standards.\
 nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261007
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261007
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f93982874561796a@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261006T223918Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261006
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Board of Occupational Safety and Health to Ho
 st Listening Sessions on Proposed Indoor Air Quality Compliance Directive
DESCRIPTION:Skip First Level Navigation\nSkip All Navigation\nMaine.gov\nA
 gencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nMaine Departmen
 t of Labor\nContact Us\n|\nNews\n|\nOnline services\n|\nPublications\n|\nS
 itemap\nHome\nContact Us\nOnline Services\nAbout MDOL\nNews & Events\nForm
 s & Publications\nMDOL Bureaus\nHome\n→\nNews & Events\n→ Board of Occ
 upational Safety and Health to Host Listening Sessions on Proposed Indoor 
 Air Quality Compliance Directive\nServices\nJobs & Training\nUnemployment 
 Insurance\nDisability Services\nWorkplace Safety\nLabor Laws\nLabor Statis
 tics\nWorkforce Development\nBoard of Occupational Safety and Health to Ho
 st Listening Sessions on Proposed Indoor Air Quality Compliance Directive\
 nOctober 6\, 2026\nFor Immediate Release\nMedia Contact: Autumn Mahoney\, 
 Communications Specialist\nmedia.dol@maine.gov\nAUGUSTA - The Board of Occ
 upational Safety and Health (BOSH) and the Maine Department of Labor Burea
 u of Labor Standards (BLS) Workplace Safety and Health Division invite int
 erested parties and the public to participate in listening sessions on a\n
 proposed voluntary compliance directive on indoor air quality\n.\nBOSH reg
 ulates and enforces workplace safety and health rules that apply to public
  employers\, including state and local governmental agencies\, school dist
 ricts and quasi-governmental agencies. BOSH is considering publishing a vo
 luntary compliance directive to help public employers monitor and maintain
  healthy indoor air quality in public workplaces. The proposed compliance 
 directive is based in part on the research and recommendations of the Indo
 or Air Quality Advisory Group in 2025.\nBOSH and BLS will host the followi
 ng listening sessions:\nOct. 13\, 2026\, at 1 p.m. at the SafetyWorks! Tra
 ining Institute\, 45 Commerce Dr.\, Augusta\nNov. 2\, 2026\, at 3 p.m. by 
 Zoom with a focus on public school and education employers\nhttps://maines
 tate.zoom.us/j/82302635655?pwd=b1nVGHTd2yeypvjPXwaJlAkK2YhjkL.1\nNov. 9\, 
 2026\, at 10 a.m. by Zoom\nhttps://mainestate.zoom.us/j/89708408033?pwd=XN
 Ua0qqLPRc\nhttp://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?id=1336377
